From 460fab73bc0d621a4afc0ba220ea7c13f2442a2a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PyScotch <pyscotch@noreply.invalid>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:03:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/8] Bugfix: account for variable weights of zero-degree
 variables in `hallOrderHdR2Halmd()` [report PyScotch]

Variables without off-diagonal non-zeros are eliminated at once before
the main loop, but the number of eliminated variables was incremented
by one instead of by their weight, while the total is weighted. When
such a variable has a weight greater than one, as happens for isolated
vertices of weighted graphs or for compressed mesh nodes, the main
loop runs out of pivots and the routine aborts with "internal error
(2)". The default mesh ordering strategy fails this way on any mesh
made of elements with private nodes. `hallOrderHfR3Hamdf4()` already
does the right thing.

Add a weighted graph with isolated vertices to the `gord` and `gotst`
program checks, and a mesh of disjoint triangles to
`test_scotch_mesh_order`.

Refs: PS-SEP-4
